17.1  free vs available

TL;DR
  - free: memory nobody is using at all, not even as cache.
  - available: what your apps can still get without swapping.
  - Look at available, not free: the available column of the Mem: line in
    free -h.

      free -h

On your machine
  RAM       10.5 GiB available of 15.5 GiB (68%) · 1.1 GiB completely free
  Cache     7.5 GiB in buff/cache, 604.0 MiB of it shared (tmpfs)
  Swap      256.0 MiB used of 8.0 GiB · swappiness 60

Pitfalls
  - "free" near zero is normal: Linux fills spare RAM with page cache (file data
    kept in RAM so it doesn't have to be read from disk again).
  - That cache is handed back as soon as an app needs the memory.
  - Not all of buff/cache can be handed back: files in tmpfs, like /dev/shm,
    live there too. "available" already accounts for that.
  - A little swap in use is not a problem by itself. What hurts is constant
    swapping: watch the si and so columns of vmstat 1.

Deep dive: 3 questions. Read them with idk 17.1 --why

Related
  Commands  free, vmstat
  Paths     /proc/meminfo
  Hub       idk memory Memory
  See also  idk 17.3 Swap and swappiness
  See also  idk 17.2 The OOM killer
